i'm going to take you live to dublin now  where shin fein now the largest party in  the northern ireland assembly for the  first time is holding a press conference  we have their assembled mary lou  mcdonald michelle o'neill let's have a  listen now need to be up and running the  executive and the assembly it is a  matter of disgrace that the dup it has  to be said with the support and  connivance of the british government  have moved to frustrate the democratic  will of the people there has to be a  respect for the democratic outcome of  that election there has to be a respect  for people's votes  and it has to be recognized that there  is very very serious work to be done i  mean we are in the grip of a cost of  living crisis people are literally  struggling to meet their grocery bills  to keep their their homes uh heat and uh  lit  and we are clear that we need and we are  insistent that there is a functioning  government in the north that we don't  simply have a caretaker ministers in in  key positions but we actually have  people who can act and take decisions  now to put money into people's pockets  to make the necessary investment in our  health services  and you know  boris johnson can't play games with the  people of the north or with the island  of ireland  he has connived with the dup  to use ireland to use the north of  ireland to use  unionism in ireland as a pawn in a wider  game that is being played out  with the european union and that is  clearly a shameful tactic and approach  and it's one that is absolutely not  acceptable  we'll meet boris johnson on monday  in the north  and i know that uh michelle as as first  minister i i hope uh we hope will meet  with the taoiseach in in the course of  the coming week also michelle do you  want to add anything or connor we will  go to questions  what exactly are you going to do next  about this then obviously you described  as disgraceful as a situation around  this dormant assembly  is it time then for perhaps you know the  irish government for the us government  for the eu to step in and put additional  pressure then on the british government  to make sure that things can move on  well look it's it's time for all hands  on deck michelle led our team yesterday  into the assembly we committed in the  course of the campaign that on day one  that shin fein would turn up ready for  work ready to do the business and we  have been as good as our word and what  we simply want is for the outcome the  natural outworking of the election to  take shape the establishment of an  executive the nomination of first  minister of deputy first minister the  nomination and appointment of a speaker  i mean the dup have not simply uh called  a hold to the formation of an executive  they have equally  attempted to place a vita on the  operation of the assembly i mean it's it  is outrageous  um and the british government have  assisted the dup  in these blocking tactics and they need  to desist and certainly when we meet  boris johnson on monday we will be  making that very clear to him i think  the response internationally to these uh  tactics shameful tactics by the dep has  been um  to call them out and to say very clearly  that it is not just the will of the  people in the north to have progress and  an inclusive executive and a first  minister for all but that is the demand  of ireland in its totality and indeed  our international partners  meet boris johnson on one day that you  could actually be facing disaster you  could be facing the prime minister who  is going to announce that  unilaterally legislation  look we've been here before paul um as  you know is the the british government  throughout the the process of of brexit  and the negotiation and lester b said  the ratification of of the protocol have  not acted in good faith they have  consistently um threatened to act and  have acted unilaterally and let's just  be clear the protocol is going nowhere  the protocol is a necessary outworking  of brexit for which uh the the tory  party and the dup campaigns  um  and uh  the british government cannot use  ireland uh as a pawn we won't be the  collateral damage in in the the brexit  negotiations our priority is to get the  institutions up and running i don't  think that anybody should be brought  back or spooked  by um more actions or rhetoric of bad  faith from the british government i  think we're all well acquainted with  that uh now and what the british  government embarrassed johnson needs to  understand is that they may wish to  try and thwart or delay or stall change  and progressivity  in ireland they will not succeed i think  it was very clear during the election  campaign  michelle what people wanted what the  message was loud and clear from people  and it was for partnership for  constructive politics  and above all else in the here and now a  response  to the really really difficult  circumstances in which people find  themselves so yes we have heard the  message from london  they are saber rattling they're engaging  in a game of brinkmanship we have been  here before we have said before and we  reiterate uh again that the people of  this island deserve so much better than  that and boris johnson and his  government cannot play these dangerous  games uh with ireland  and we will continue to demand that the  the outcome of the election is  acknowledged and respected  do you believe you'll actually get a  crack at the office of first minister  before the end of the summer but it does  here at all i mean what's your elephant  heading sir  there should be no delay we were there  yesterday we turned up as we promised  the electorate throughout the campaign  itself that we would turn up on day one  that we'd work to put money in in their  pockets because people are struggling  with the cost of living crisis people  are our health services on its knees and  needs fixed so we're there to do  business we want to work in partnership  with others but the dup failed to turn  up the field on day one to actually form  an executive let us get back to work do  what we need to do for the public the  public voted in strong strong numbers  probably the largest election turnout  that we've seen in a long time but they  voted for politics to work they voted  for parties to work together but the dup  have done two things they're punishing  the public for their own brexit mess  they're being facilitated in that by the  tories so when boris johnson comes here  comes to belfast on monday we will meet  with them but we put it very strongly to  them they are punished in the public and  that is not acceptable and boris johnson  has no mandate here on the island of  ireland but yet not he's facilitating  this dup madness at a time whenever the  people that need us to be there for them  so um i want the opportunity to lead i  want the opportunity to be in the office  of first minister i think it's about the  future it demonstrates the real change  that actually is happening in our  society but people need politics to work  and i'm going to continue to work at  that every day because that's what the  election said  the response from the dep is that there  is  a mandate to stay out  they have received that mandate and on  that basis then that country will turn  them to the issues with both what have  been resolved  i think the are being dishonest with the  public they fought this election on a  five-point plan and they've failed to  turn up on day one they are blocking  progress they're blocking an executive  be informed they're blocking a  government that can actually put money  in people's pockets they're blocking us  starting to fix the health service the  rest of the parties were there yesterday  to do business together so when the dup  talk about consensus they actually mean  consensus on their terms they actually  mean they do use pa p's way or no way  that's not acceptable we didn't consent  to brexit but it's been fostered upon us  the protocol is here to stay so there  needs to be an insertion of honesty into  the conversation the protocol is here to  stay are there ways to smooth its  implementation yes and we're up for that  but that should not block the  establishment of an executive  if it is the case we head into the  unilateral territory which has been  threatened for so long for making plans  it does bring back the possibility of a  hardcore it does bring back that as a  real thing you meant to use the words  and dangerous brickmanship game that's  being played  can you quantify what the level of  danger you think it is because suddenly  you just always get very real again  people might have thought it would have  gone away  no i mean it is very dangerous it's  reckless it's it's a game of  brinkmanship very cynically carried out  by a tory government in london that has  no care for the island of ireland north  or south  where we need to be very much focused is  on protecting irish interests north and  south east and west that's our job to  protect the interests of everybody who  calls this island home and our clear  interest is to ensure that the protocol  is implemented in a way that is smooth  in a way that ensures that business can  work in in a way that ensures that we  that that everybody benefits um so i  would just caution against anybody being  overly spooked or distracted by the kind  of the loud rhetoric from boris johnson  we need to keep our eye here on the  prize and the prize is to establish  functioning institutions good government  but also to ensure that the the outcome  of brexit does not negatively harm the  island of ireland that's where our focus  is we meet boris johnson on monday  we'll set out the stall very very  clearly uh to him we're not one bit  naive as to what's happening here it is  very clear that the tory government in  london is in cahoots with the dup to  stall  and to hold back progress  to frustrate the will of the people as  expressed in the election and that to  anybody who calls themselves a democrat  is clearly unacceptable and clearly  shameful and that case will be made to  boris johnson and i have no doubt that  the government in dublin that our  partners at a european level and indeed  across the atlantic in the united states  of america will reiterate that message  loud and clear  national maternity hospital earlier on  this morning and the further and higher  education minister simon harris  indicated that it was likely there could  be some form of codicil memorandum  addendum stuck into any deal which would  try and clarify those two difficult  words  which we've heard about would that be  something you'd welcome i i think um  clarity on on that question is  undoubtedly very welcome i think people  want to know that all  lawful services will be provided to to  women um in the new maternity hospital  and that the  the will of the woman is is uh respected  fully so i think that is welcome  obviously we await uh  seeing what what the the wording or the  the content of this catalog  addendum might be um but there is still  the outstanding issue of ownership you  know that that is still a very very  vexed question and the need for a public  hospital which will  um cost very considerable amounts of  public monies being cited on public land  and that question unfortunately the the  government have proved unwilling to even  entertain what i think is a very  reasonable suggestion and that is that  the t shook the tarnish then the  minister for health sit down with the  saint vincent healthcare group  speak to them and  press for a solution which is a clean  transfer of the site of the land  to the state after all that was what was  in  initially envisaged so i i would urge  not just a resolution of one issue but  resolution of the second issue which is  the question of ownership of the site  we're going to leave that conference  there mary lou mcdonald michelle o'neill  from

